"Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42275490/)\nciteturn20search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure\n\n. 2026 Jun 11;392(6803):1183-1187.\n\ndoi: 10.1126/science.aed5051. Epub 2026 Jun 11.\n\n# Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure\n\nJeongeun Ryu^{  1 }, Mathieu Colombani^{  1 }, Corentin Mollier^{  1 }, Joël Marthelot^{  1 }, Yoël Forterre^{  1 }\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Aix-Marseille University, CNRS, IUSTI UMR 7343 and Turing Center for Living Systems, Marseille, France.\n\n  * PMID: 42275490\n  * DOI: 10.1126/science.aed5051\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure\n\nJeongeun Ryu et al. Science. 2026.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\n. 2026 Jun 11;392(6803):1183-1187.\n\ndoi: 10.1126/science.aed5051. Epub 2026 Jun 11.\n\n### Authors\n\nJeongeun Ryu^{  1 }, Mathieu Colombani^{  1 }, Corentin Mollier^{  1 }, Joël Marthelot^{  1 }, Yoël Forterre^{  1 }\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Aix-Marseille University, CNRS, IUSTI UMR 7343 and Turing Center for Living Systems, Marseille, France.\n\n  * PMID: 42275490\n  * DOI: 10.1126/science.aed5051\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n[Button: Full text links ]Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat [Select]\n\n## Abstract\n\nPlants can move rapidly without muscles, as seen in the Venus flytrap's snapping lobes-a long-standing puzzle in plant biomechanics. Trap closure involves an elastic instability, but the active mechanical driver has remained elusive. Using in situ hydraulic and mechanical measurements, we identified the motor driving this transition. Closure occurs too quickly to be explained by water transport, revealing a distinct, nonhydraulic mechanism: a rapid (about one second) softening of the epidermal cell wall, releasing elastic energy stored in the trap. This represents the fastest modulation of wall mechanics reported in plants. Our finding reveals a mode of plant motility based on dynamic tuning of material properties, suggesting principles for muscle-free, bioinspired actuation.\n\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * [Button: Biomechanical Phenomena ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Cell Wall* / physiology ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Droseraceae* / physiology ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Elasticity ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Movement ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Plant Epidermis / physiology ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Water / metabolism ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n## Substances\n\n  * [Button: Water ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure – Yoël Forterre (https://yoelforterre.wordpress.com/2026/06/18/fast-cell-wall-softening-causes-venus-flytrap-closure/)\nciteturn20search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last month; # Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure ... Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure.\n\n# Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure\n\nOur paper on the Venus flytrap, with Jeongeun Ryu, Mathieu Colombani, Corentin Mollier, and Joël Marthelot, has just been published in Science and is featured on the cover!\n\nJ. Ryu, M. Colombani, C. Mollier, J. Marthelot, Y. Forterre. Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure. Science, 2026, 392 (6803), pp.1183-1187\n\nImage\n\nThe carnivorous Venus flytrap is an iconic example of rapid plant movement and has fascinated scientists and the public alike since Darwin’s time. Although its ultrafast trap closure is known to involve an elastic instability, analogous to the snap-through buckling of a thin shell, the active mechanism triggering this motion has remained elusive.\n\nIn this study, we combine multiscale in situ mechanical and hydraulic measurements—from the whole organ down to the subcellular level—to show that trap closure is powered by a rapid and localized softening of the outer epidermal cell wall, which releases elastic energy stored in the tissue. This softening occurs within just a few seconds and results in a 40% decrease in wall stiffness, representing what is likely the fastest modulation of plant cell wall mechanics reported to date.\n\nThese findings challenge conventional models based on osmotic flow and hydraulic transport and provide a new framework for understanding rapid plant movements, with potential applications in bioinspired actuators, soft robotics, and smart materials.\n\nLink for downloading the published version of the paper: https://www.science.org/eprint/6MSZFZC9CCTACUE6SFFZ/full?activationRedirect=/doi/full/10.1126/science.aed5051\n\nAccepted version on HAL, including the methods and the supplementary information: https://hal.science/view/index/docid/5658677\n\nImage: Unknown's avatar yoelforterreJune 18, 2026June 18, 2026\n\n## One thought on “Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure”\n\n  1. Pingback: Mucholapka nefunguje jako hydraulika. Nová studie odhalila, že past spouští sekundové změknutí buněčných stěn - VědaŽivě.cz--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nData supporting the publication \"Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure\" (https://explore.openaire.eu/search/result?pid=10.5281%2Fzenodo.20426165)\nciteturn20search